var POOL = [
{p:2,m:"Security",t:"Provider can't sign orders after department transfer",d:"Hospitalist moved from Med/Surg to ICU service last week; can open charts but the Sign button is grayed out for all order types.",
tri:["Is it all order types or specific ones? All = user security; specific = order-level build. This one question halves the search space.","Did anything change recently? (Yes — department transfer. In access tickets, 'what changed' almost always is the answer wearing a disguise.)","Does a peer in the same new role sign fine? A known-good comparison target turns guessing into diffing."],
res:["Open the user's <code>EMP</code> record and the linked <code>SER</code> provider record — confirm the SER is active and credentialed for the new service.","Run a user comparison against a working ICU peer: diff security class, template, and subtemplate line by line.","Department transfer workflows commonly reassign the template but drop a module-specific security class (e.g., order signing authority). Restore only the missing class — the minimum delta, nothing broader.","Test with the provider or via user-context testing in TST; paste the before/after diff into the ticket work notes so the next analyst inherits the pattern."],
kn:"<p><strong>EMP vs SER is the first distinction every Epic analyst internalizes.</strong> EMP is the <em>user</em> record: login, security classes, templates, In Basket setup — everything about the person as a system user. SER is the <em>provider</em> record: credentials, specialties, schedulable resources, ordering authority — everything about the person as a clinician. Signing lives at their intersection: a perfectly valid SER attached to an EMP with a broken security class still can't sign, and vice versa.</p><p>Security itself is layered and additive: a security class is a bundle of individual security points; templates group classes for a role; subtemplates specialize them. The practical consequence: two users 'in the same role' can differ by one class buried three layers deep, which is why user comparison — not reading documentation — is the fastest diagnostic. Every security change is audited, so precision matters twice: for least-privilege and for the audit trail you leave behind.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The tempting shortcut:</strong> granting a broader template because 'it works now.' That's privilege creep — you've traded a 10-minute diff for a permanent audit finding and possible HIPAA exposure.","<strong>Scope miss:</strong> fixing this provider without asking whether the transfer workflow itself drops the class — if HR moved four hospitalists this month, you have three more tickets incoming. Fix the instance, then question the pattern.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"For access issues I compare against a known-good user, change the minimum delta, and document the diff — least privilege isn't a policy, it's a habit.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Security",t:"New-hire nurse can't see any patients on her unit",d:"Started Monday, logs in fine, but her patient list is empty while coworkers see the full unit census.",
tri:["Can she open a chart via direct MRN search? If yes, access exists — this is a list/context problem, not a security problem. Never conflate 'can't find' with 'can't access.'","What login department is she selecting at sign-in — and what departments does her profile actually offer?","Was her position provisioned against the right unit, or against a generic new-hire default?"],
res:["Check the EMP login department(s) — new hires are frequently provisioned to a default or placeholder <code>DEP</code> that doesn't match their unit.","Verify her system patient list configuration pulls the unit census (unit-based lists key off the login department).","Fix the department association, have her fully log out and back in (context is set at login), and watch the census populate together — closing access tickets 'on faith' generates reopens.","If the provisioning feed set the wrong DEP, flag it to the identity/provisioning team: one wrong new hire usually means a template is wrong for the whole cohort."],
kn:"<p><strong>Login department is the invisible hand of the Epic session.</strong> The department chosen at sign-in shapes patient lists, printer routing, order defaults, preference lists, and even which activities appear on screen. It's why the same user can log into two departments and see what feels like two different systems — and why 'it works at my other clinic' is a diagnostic clue, not a complaint.</p><p>Upstream of Epic sits the provisioning pipeline: HR system → identity management → EMP creation from role templates. Most day-one access tickets are born there, not in Epic — a job code mapped to the wrong template, a department that didn't exist when the template was built. Analysts who learn to read the provisioning chain resolve new-hire tickets at the root instead of hand-patching every Monday's cohort. This pipeline is also, not coincidentally, the exact subject matter of an IAM analyst role.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The tempting shortcut:</strong> manually building her a patient list. It works today and hides the broken provisioning that will misfire for every future hire in that role.","<strong>Diagnostic trap:</strong> jumping to security classes when chart-by-MRN works fine — you'll burn an hour in the wrong layer. Access vs visibility, always separate them first.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Day-one tickets are provisioning tickets until proven otherwise — I fix the user, then check whether the template that created the user is the real patient.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Security",t:"Physician keeps getting locked out despite correct password",d:"Badge tap works at some workstations but locks his account at two specific WOWs in the ED.",
tri:["User-wide or workstation-specific? Two specific WOWs = endpoint/SSO layer; everywhere = AD or Epic. Scope IS the diagnosis here.","Badge tap vs manually typed password — which path fails? They traverse different components.","When did the two WOWs last get agent updates or re-imaging?"],
res:["Recognize the layer: this is the SSO tier (Imprivata), not Epic authentication. Check badge-to-account enrollment and the agent version on the two failing WOWs.","The classic mechanism: a stale cached credential on a shared workstation silently replays an old password against AD until the lockout threshold trips — the user did nothing wrong.","Clear the cached credential store / re-enroll the badge on those endpoints; coordinate agent updates with the desktop team.","Write the layer boundary into the resolution notes — 'lockouts scoped to specific endpoints = SSO cache, route to endpoint team' — so the next occurrence routes right in one hop."],
kn:"<p><strong>The authentication chain is: badge tap → Imprivata SSO → Active Directory → Epic.</strong> A lockout can originate at any link, and each has a signature: user-locked-everywhere points at AD (or a mobile device replaying old credentials — the phone in the pocket is a notorious offender); user-locked-at-specific-machines points at endpoint agents or credential caches; Epic-side inactivity or security issues don't lock AD at all. Triage by scope and you skip three wrong teams.</p><p>Shared clinical workstations make this worse by design: kiosk-mode WOWs cycle dozens of users a shift, credential caches accumulate, and AD lockout policies (e.g., 5 bad attempts / 30 minutes) interact badly with anything replaying stale secrets. This seam — where clinical workflow meets identity infrastructure — is precisely where Epic analyst work and IAM work overlap, and being fluent on both sides of it is rare enough to be a career differentiator.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The hamster wheel:</strong> resetting the password repeatedly without finding the replay source — the lockout returns in 30 minutes and the physician's trust in IT drops each cycle.","<strong>Routing failure:</strong> bouncing the ticket between Epic team and desktop team because nobody names the layer. Name the chain in your notes; ambiguity is what makes tickets ping-pong.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"I triage auth issues by scope: everywhere = directory, some machines = endpoint/SSO cache, one app = that app. Knowing the chain means I fix in minutes or route in one hop.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Security",t:"Break-the-glass firing on a routine patient",d:"Front-desk staff report a BTG warning when opening a chart for a scheduled clinic patient; workflow is slowed and they're worried they did something wrong.",
tri:["Is the patient flagged as restricted/confidential — employee, VIP, behavioral health, research participant? Check the flag before assuming malfunction.","Is this user's role expected to have unrestricted access to this population, or is the prompt exactly what policy intends?","Is the real issue the prompt, or that staff don't know what to do when it appears?"],
res:["Check the patient-level restriction flag — most of the time it's set correctly (a hospital employee being seen as a patient is the everyday case).","If legitimate: this is education, not repair. Walk staff through the attestation workflow — select the true reason, proceed; access is logged, not punished, when the reason is valid care.","If the flag is genuinely in error: route to HIM/privacy to evaluate and remove. Analysts never remove privacy restrictions on their own authority, however obvious it looks.","Leave the clinic a one-paragraph 'when you see BTG' explainer — the ticket recurs because the knowledge is missing, not because the system is broken."],
kn:"<p><strong>Break-the-glass is HIPAA's minimum-necessary principle rendered as a workflow.</strong> Restricted populations — employees, public figures, behavioral health, sometimes research cohorts — get an extra gate: state your reason, get access, get logged. The design accepts that emergencies can't wait for permission and instead moves enforcement to the back end: the privacy office runs BTG audit reports and investigates the attestations that don't hold up.</p><p>The cultural piece matters as much as the config: staff who fear BTG start asking colleagues to open charts for them, which is worse for privacy and destroys the audit trail's meaning. The analyst's real deliverable on these tickets is often confidence — the control is working, your legitimate access is defensible, here's the workflow. And the boundary is bright: the flag itself belongs to HIM/privacy governance; analysts implement, never adjudicate.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The career-limiting move:</strong> removing a privacy flag yourself because it 'seems wrong.' If the patient turns out to be flagged for a reason you couldn't see, that's your name on the change log.","<strong>The quiet harm:</strong> coaching users toward a generic attestation reason to 'make the prompt go away' — you've just taught them to falsify an audit record.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Half of security tickets are controls working as designed on users who were never taught the workflow — my job is to fix the config when it's wrong and fix the understanding when it isn't.\"</span>"]},

{p:2,m:"ClinDoc",t:"Vitals from bedside monitors not filing to flowsheets",d:"One ICU pod's monitors show data locally but nothing crosses into Epic since a monitor firmware update last night.",
tri:["Scope: one bed, one pod, or house-wide? One pod immediately after a firmware update — the correlation is practically a confession.","Are device-to-bed associations still intact, or did the update reset them?","Is nursing aware they must chart vitals manually until this is fixed? The workaround has to be running before the fix does."],
res:["Confirm manual charting is happening first — the clinical safety net precedes the technical fix.","Check the device integration gateway (e.g., Capsule/medical device hub): firmware updates can change outbound message formats or identifiers, breaking parsing at the gateway.","Verify device concept mapping — each monitor parameter (HR, SpO2, arterial line pressures) must map to its exact flowsheet row ID; a firmware change can shift the parameter labels the mapping keys on.","Coordinate with biomed on rollback vs mapping update; then validate every bed in the pod and every parameter type — not one bed, one vital — before declaring resolution. Partial mappings are the sneakiest failure mode."],
kn:"<p><strong>Device data travels a four-hop pipeline: monitor → integration gateway → interface → flowsheet row.</strong> Each hop has a different owner (biomed, integration platform team, interface team, Epic analyst) and a break at any hop looks identical from the bedside — the nurse just sees no data. Analysts who can name the hops and test each one independently turn a three-team finger-pointing session into a 30-minute isolation exercise.</p><p>The stakes are quiet but real: validated device data feeds clinical decisions, early-warning scores, and sepsis alerts. A <em>silent</em> filing failure is worse than a loud one because downstream logic keeps computing on stale numbers. This is also why device integration changes are supposed to ride change control with clinical engineering validation — a firmware push that skipped downstream impact review is the process finding to raise in the ticket's wake, not just the mapping you patched.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The premature victory:</strong> testing one bed, one parameter, closing the ticket. Firmware changes break mappings unevenly — the pod's SpO2 can file while its arterial lines don't.","<strong>The ownership dodge:</strong> 'that's a biomed problem.' It's a shared pipeline; the analyst who convenes the three owners is the one leadership remembers.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"For device integration I think in hops — monitor, gateway, interface, flowsheet — and test each independently. And the workaround starts before the diagnosis does: manual charting first, root cause second.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"ClinDoc",t:"New pressure-injury protocol needs a flowsheet row",d:"Wound care committee approved a new assessment scale; nursing education asks for it to be documented in the existing skin assessment flowsheet.",
tri:["Is there a signed-off definition — exact scale values, assessment frequency, which units? Build without a spec is rework with extra steps.","Does an existing row already capture this? Duplicate rows are permanent reporting pollution.","Who consumes this data downstream — quality reporting, wound care registry? Their requirements shape the value set."],
res:["Reclassify honestly: this is an enhancement, not break/fix. Confirm nursing documentation committee approval is actually on record, not just asserted.","Search existing flowsheet rows first — retiring or extending beats duplicating.","Build the row(s) with the approved value set in POC, attach to the correct flowsheet group and template for the target units, and validate in TST <em>with the requester driving</em>.","Schedule the Data Courier migration against the change calendar, synchronized with nursing education's rollout date — a row that appears before staff are trained generates its own ticket wave."],
kn:"<p><strong>Flowsheets are a build hierarchy: rows (the data element) → groups (clinical groupings) → templates (assigned to units/roles).</strong> A row's ID is forever — every value ever charted against it keys to that ID in Clarity and Caboodle, which is why duplicating an existing concept fragments years of reporting and why 'just add a row' deserves more caution than it gets. Naming conventions and value-set discipline aren't bureaucracy; they're what keeps the warehouse queryable.</p><p>The deeper principle: documentation build is clinical policy made executable. The committee decides <em>what</em> is assessed and <em>how often</em>; the analyst decides <em>how</em> it's structured for capture and downstream use. Blurring that line — building what a single requester asked for without governance — creates shadow documentation standards, and unwinding those later costs 10x the meeting you skipped.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The eager-to-help trap:</strong> building on a verbal request because the requester is credible. Six months later quality reporting finds two rows measuring the same thing and neither is authoritative.","<strong>The invisible stakeholder:</strong> forgetting that flowsheet data feeds registries and dashboards — a value set that reads fine at the bedside can be unaggregatable in the warehouse.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"I treat documentation build as data architecture: every row is a permanent column in the org's clinical dataset, so governance sign-off and downstream-consumer review come before I touch POC.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"ClinDoc",t:"SmartPhrase pulling stale problem-list data into notes",d:"Cardiology reports .CARDCONSULT inserts an outdated problem list even after the chart is updated.",
tri:["System-level SmartPhrase or someone's personal copy? Ownership determines both the fix and who else is affected.","Stale at insertion, or correct at insertion but the note wasn't refreshed after chart updates? Different mechanisms, different education.","Which SmartLink variant is embedded — active problems, encounter problems, or a historical snapshot link?"],
res:["Open the SmartPhrase and audit every embedded SmartLink — 'wrong data in my note' is almost always a link problem inside a phrase, not a phrase problem.","Identify the offending link: an old variant, a link pulling encounter-context problems when the user expects the active list, or a deprecated token that fails quietly.","Swap in the current system SmartLink; test in TST against a chart with <em>recent</em> problem-list changes — testing against a static chart proves nothing.","If it's a personal copy forked from the system phrase: fix the system version, then teach the user to re-point — personal forks never inherit fixes and rot independently."],
kn:"<p><strong>The SmartTools family, in one breath:</strong> SmartPhrases are dot-phrase expansions; SmartTexts are larger structured templates; SmartLinks are the live-data tokens (the @-syntax) that pull chart content at insertion; SmartLists are constrained choice lists. They compose — phrases contain links and lists — which means a defect surfaces at the phrase level but lives at the link level, and debugging means decomposing.</p><p>Context is the subtle killer: a SmartLink resolves against a context (patient, encounter, order) at the moment of insertion, so the 'same' link can return different data in a note versus a letter versus an In Basket reply. And the ownership model matters operationally: system tools are maintained centrally and inherit fixes; personal copies are user-owned forks. Power users who copied a system phrase in 2023 are running 2023 logic today — a personalization session that re-points them is worth a dozen individual tickets.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The whack-a-mole fix:</strong> editing one user's personal phrase and closing. Four more cardiologists have the same stale fork; fix the system source and migrate the users.","<strong>The context blind spot:</strong> testing the link in a different activity than where the user works — it can pass your test and fail their workflow legitimately.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"SmartTool tickets I debug by decomposition — phrase, then links inside it, then the context each link resolves against. And I always ask 'system or personal copy' first, because it changes who else is silently affected.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Orders",t:"New lab test missing from the sepsis order set",d:"Lab validated a new procalcitonin assay two weeks ago; ED physicians can order it standalone but it's absent from the sepsis bundle.",
tri:["Standalone ordering works — so the <code>EAP</code> record exists and is active. The gap is in the order set's content, which narrows this to build + governance.","Has the addition actually been approved by whoever owns the sepsis order set clinically, or is 'lab validated it' being conflated with 'the committee approved it'?","Pre-checked or optional in the set? That's a clinical utilization decision, not an analyst preference."],
res:["Confirm the EAP orderable's status and ED-context availability (already proven by standalone orders — document it anyway).","Verify or obtain order set committee / physician champion approval, including the pre-checked-vs-optional decision in writing.","Build the addition in POC exactly per the approved spec, validate in TST with an ED physician if possible, and courier per the change calendar — order set changes are visible enough to warrant announced timing.","Close the loop with the original requester and lab: the gap between 'assay live' and 'assay in the pathway' was two weeks of sub-optimal ordering; note whether intake missed it."],
kn:"<p><strong>An orderable existing is not an orderable appearing.</strong> The <code>EAP</code> master file defines the procedure; order sets, preference lists, quick lists, and protocols each reference it separately. Adding a test to the catalog wires nothing downstream — every ordering surface is its own build decision. This is why 'the lab says it's live but doctors can't find it' is one of the most common ticket genres in existence.</p><p>Order sets deserve extra respect because their defaults <em>steer medicine</em>: a pre-checked item gets ordered dramatically more often than an optional one — a nudge effect measured repeatedly in CDS research. That makes checkbox state a clinical and stewardship decision (cost, antimicrobial exposure, blood utilization), owned by governance and merely implemented by the analyst. The analyst's leverage is making the decision explicit: 'pre-checked or optional?' asked in writing has prevented more utilization incidents than any amount of build skill.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The silent nudge:</strong> adding it pre-checked because the requester said 'just add it' — congratulations, you personally raised procalcitonin utilization 40% without a committee ever deciding to.","<strong>The intake gap:</strong> fixing this instance without asking why a validated new assay didn't automatically trigger an order-set review — that process hole will reopen with the next new test.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Order set defaults are clinical policy with a checkbox UI — I implement exactly what governance approved, and if the approval doesn't specify checked-versus-optional, I ask rather than decide.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Orders",t:"Physicians demand a med interaction alert be silenced",d:"A hospitalist group says a specific drug-pair BPA fires constantly on a combination they co-prescribe deliberately, and they're clicking through without reading.",
tri:["Pull the alert analytics before forming any opinion: firing volume, override rate, override reasons, and which provider groups trip it.","Is the interaction clinically meaningful for <em>this</em> population per pharmacy — or a textbook interaction that inpatient monitoring already mitigates?","Can firing criteria be narrowed (context, service, dose thresholds) rather than binary on/off?"],
res:["Resist the unilateral silence — gather the evidence: an alert overridden 96% of the time with reason 'aware, will monitor' is data, and data is what governance acts on.","Take findings to the CDS/alert governance committee with pharmacy at the table; the option space is richer than on/off — restrict to specific contexts, add dose conditions, exclude the service that co-manages deliberately.","Implement exactly the committee's decision, then <em>measure again</em>: post-change override rate is the success metric, and reporting it back is what makes the next tuning request easier.","Tell the requesting hospitalists what changed and why — physicians who see their feedback produce measured change file better feedback next time."],
kn:"<p><strong>Alert fatigue is a patient-safety pathology with a number attached:</strong> published override rates for interruptive med alerts routinely exceed 90%, meaning the alert protects nobody while actively training clinicians to dismiss <em>all</em> alerts — including the one that would have caught a fatal error. Every noisy alert spends down a finite attention budget. This is why 'just silence it' and 'never silence anything' are both wrong: the discipline is measurement, targeted tuning, and re-measurement.</p><p>BPA (BestPractice Advisory) build gives you the tuning levers: firing criteria (who, where, what context), lockout periods (don't re-fire for N hours), acknowledgment reasons (which become your analytics), and display modes (interruptive vs passive). The analyst's superpower on these tickets is arriving at governance with override data instead of opinions — it converts a physician-vs-IT argument into a shared optimization problem, and it's one of the highest-visibility things a CSA does.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The favor that becomes a finding:</strong> silencing globally because a vocal group asked — when the interaction eventually harms a patient on a different service, the audit trail shows an analyst, not a committee, made a clinical decision.","<strong>The half-finished job:</strong> tuning the alert and never measuring after — without the post-change override rate you can't prove the change helped, and governance stops trusting the process.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Alert requests get the same treatment as performance problems: measure, tune the narrowest lever, measure again. Override rate above 90% means the alert is training people to ignore alerts — that framing usually gets the committee's attention.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Orders",t:"STAT labs defaulting to Routine in one clinic",d:"Providers in a specialty clinic report priority defaults to Routine even when they intend STAT, causing lab delays.",
tri:["One clinic only — so the orderable's base default is fine and this lives in a department or personal layer. Scope has already done half the diagnosis.","Are affected providers ordering from a department preference list entry with a saved Routine priority?","Any recent preference list maintenance in that department?"],
res:["Inspect the department preference list entries for the affected labs: saved defaults (priority, collection, frequency) silently override the orderable's base configuration.","Diff against a clinic that behaves correctly — same technique as user comparison, applied to build.","Correct the department entry; then the often-missed step: individual providers' <em>personal</em> preference lists may have captured the stale default and will keep 'reverting' the fix from the users' perspective.","Send the clinic a one-liner on what changed — 'the system keeps doing it' tickets die when users understand which layer was at fault."],
kn:"<p><strong>Order defaults cascade: personal preference list → department preference list → orderable (EAP) base.</strong> The closest layer to the user wins, which is efficient by design — frequent orders pre-filled with each clinic's norms — and maddening in failure, because the provider sees only the final result and reasonably blames 'the system.' Layer-aware triage ('one user / one department / everywhere') maps directly onto this cascade and is the fastest path through any defaulting ticket.</p><p>Preference lists are also a quiet safety surface: a saved default of Routine on a lab a clinic uses for urgent rule-outs is a delay engine that fires on every order. That's why priority defaults deserve the same review discipline as order set checkboxes — they're small build decisions with cumulative clinical consequences, and they tend to be set once by whoever built the list years ago and never revisited.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The recurring ghost:</strong> fixing the department list but not the personal lists that copied the bad default — the ticket reopens in a week and now you look like you didn't fix it.","<strong>Anchoring on the orderable:</strong> spending an hour in the EAP record when one-clinic scope already told you the answer was in a preference layer.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Defaulting issues I map to the cascade — personal list, department list, orderable base — and scope tells me which layer before I open a single record.\"</span>"]},

{p:2,m:"Willow",t:"Nurses can't scan a newly stocked medication",d:"BCMA scan fails with 'medication not found' on a new manufacturer's stock of a common med; nurses are overriding to give doses on time.",
tri:["New manufacturer = new NDC = new barcode. Is that NDC mapped in the med record? (This is the answer ~90% of the time; verify anyway.)","How widespread — one unit's stock or the entire pharmacy distribution? Determines urgency and how many overrides are accumulating.","Are overrides being tracked? The override count is both your urgency metric and pharmacy's safety exposure record."],
res:["Verify the new package's NDC against the <code>ERX</code> medication record's barcode mappings — a manufacturer switch that skipped informatics is the standing root cause.","Partner with pharmacy informatics to add the mapping; then test-scan a <em>physical package from the floor</em>, not the catalog entry — packaging-level barcodes (unit-dose vs carton) have burned analysts who tested the wrong level.","Get corrected stock scanning confirmed on the reporting unit and check whether other units received the same lot.","File the process finding: purchasing/wholesaler substitutions must trigger barcode verification <em>before</em> distribution. Track and report the override volume during the gap — it's the metric that makes the process fix happen."],
kn:"<p><strong>BCMA (barcode medication administration) is the last technical control before a drug enters a patient</strong> — the scan reconciles wristband + package against the verified order to enforce the five rights: right patient, drug, dose, route, time. Every override is a manually bypassed safety check, so a scan-failure ticket where 'the med itself is fine' is still urgent: the system's protective value is exactly what's offline.</p><p>The mechanics live in the NDC (National Drug Code): manufacturer-specific, packaging-level-specific, and changed by events entirely outside IT's line of sight — wholesaler substitutions, shortages, 340B sourcing shifts. The mature org closes the loop structurally: purchasing changes feed a barcode-verification step before stock hits floors, and override-rate monitoring acts as the leading indicator when something slips through anyway. An analyst who proposes that monitoring turns one ticket into a permanent detection capability.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The normalization trap:</strong> letting 'override for now' run for days — override-heavy periods are precisely when wrong-med errors happen, because the workflow that catches them is off.","<strong>Testing the catalog, not the floor:</strong> confirming the NDC in build but never scanning the physical package — packaging-level barcode mismatches pass the desk test and fail at the bedside.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"I treat BCMA override rate as a live safety metric — a spike is my early warning that supply chain outran informatics, usually before anyone files a ticket.\"</span>"]},

{p:2,m:"Willow",t:"Smart pumps stopped receiving programmed rates from Epic",d:"Infusion orders verify normally but pumps on two units require full manual programming since this morning.",
tri:["Two units vs house-wide — do those units share a wireless segment or pump gateway? Topology-shaped scope points at infrastructure, not build.","Are pump-to-patient associations still succeeding? Association failure and programming failure implicate different components.","Confirm nursing knows the manual-programming procedure and that drug library guardrails still apply — safety posture first."],
res:["Verify the clinical safety floor: manual programming with the pump's drug library active is the designed degradation — confirm it's actually happening before diving into diagnostics.","Trace the path: verified order → interface → pump vendor server/gateway → wireless → pump. Two-unit scope suggests a gateway or wireless segment; pull biomed and network in early rather than after you've exhausted the Epic side.","Isolate the failing hop (vendor server logs and gateway status usually tell the story), coordinate the fix with the owning team.","Restore and verify end-to-end with a live test infusion on each affected unit; report the outage window and manual-programming exposure to nursing leadership."],
kn:"<p><strong>Pump interoperability is bidirectional:</strong> the verified order auto-programs infusion parameters into the pump (eliminating manual keystroke errors — the historic source of fatal infusion mistakes), and the pump auto-documents delivered volumes back into the flowsheet. Both directions ride an interface through the pump vendor's server and the hospital's wireless network, which means the failure surface includes systems Epic analysts don't own but must be able to reason about.</p><p>The design insight worth internalizing: this integration <em>degrades gracefully</em>. Pumps still enforce their onboard drug library limits during manual programming, so a failure raises error probability rather than removing protection entirely — which is why it's a P2, not a P1, and why the analyst's first verified fact should be 'the fallback is running,' not 'the interface is down.' Distinguishing failures that degrade safely from failures that don't is the judgment layer of this job.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The deprioritization error:</strong> treating it as low urgency 'because nurses can program manually' — manual keystroke error is the exact risk this integration exists to remove; every manual hour is borrowed risk.","<strong>The silo dive:</strong> exhausting Epic-side diagnostics before engaging biomed/network when two-unit topology screamed infrastructure from the first minute.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"For integrated medical devices I always establish the degradation mode first — what protection remains when the integration fails — because that determines urgency more than the outage itself does.\"</span>"]},

{p:2,m:"Beaker",t:"Specimen labels printing garbled in the ED",d:"Zebra label printers in ED triage print shifted/unreadable specimen labels; collectors are handwriting labels.",
tri:["Garbled output ≠ no output: connectivity is fine, so this is format/driver/mapping. That single observation eliminates half the diagnostic tree.","All ED label printers or specific ones? All = shared config (print server, driver package); some = local queue or hardware.","Did a print server change, driver update, or Windows patch ship in the last 24h?"],
res:["Escalate the workaround risk immediately in your head: handwritten specimen labels are a mislabeling vector — this is a P2 wearing a P3's clothes.","Check the Epic Print Services (<code>EPS</code>) queue configuration for the affected printers: label printers require label-format/driver pairings, and a print-server update that reset queues to a generic laser driver is the classic culprit.","Reassign the correct label format and driver, test-print — then <strong>verify with a barcode scanner</strong>, not your eyes. Human-readable and machine-scannable are different bars, and lab automation needs the second one.","Sweep every label queue the change touched, not just the reported ones, and log the exposure window (hours of handwritten labels) for lab quality's awareness."],
kn:"<p><strong>EPS (Epic Print Services) is a mapping engine:</strong> workstation + login department + document type resolve to a print queue, and each queue carries a format/driver pairing. Label printing is its own sub-discipline — thermal printers, ZPL-family formats, exact DPI and label-stock dimensions — where a 'nearly right' driver produces output that looks fine and scans never. That's why scanner verification is the acceptance test, full stop.</p><p>The clinical frame: specimen mislabeling is one of the laboratory's most consequential error categories — wrong-patient labels cause wrong-patient results, and the downstream harm (wrong transfusion, wrong diagnosis) is severe. Barcoded, printed-at-collection labels exist precisely to close the handwriting gap, so every hour of the workaround reopens a solved safety problem. Printing tickets read as trivial; specimen-label printing tickets never are.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The driver merry-go-round:</strong> reinstalling drivers on the endpoint repeatedly when the mapping error lives in the EPS queue — desktop-layer fixes can't repair an Epic-layer misroute (a distinction Andy can explain from both sides).","<strong>Eyeball acceptance:</strong> declaring victory on a legible test print that fails the scanner in the lab an hour later.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"I verify label fixes with a scanner, not a glance — and I treat every hour of handwritten specimen labels as accumulating mislabeling risk worth naming to lab quality.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Beaker",t:"Add-on test request rejected on an existing specimen",d:"Provider tries to add a test to this morning's blood draw; system rejects it and lab asks for a redraw the provider thinks is unnecessary.",
tri:["What exact rejection reason — stability window exceeded, insufficient volume, wrong container/additive? The reason text names the rule.","Is the build enforcing lab's actual validated policy, or has policy moved and build lagged?","Is the deliverable here a fix, or an explanation delivered well?"],
res:["Pull the test's specimen requirements in the Beaker build: stability window (analyte viability post-collection), container/additive type, minimum volume. Add-on eligibility is computed from collection time against those rules.","If build matches lab's validated procedure: the system is right. The work becomes translation — help lab or the provider's team articulate <em>why</em> (e.g., the analyte degrades in 4 hours; results after that are clinically unreliable).","If lab has re-validated a longer window and build lagged: update with lab directorship sign-off — specimen rules change only on documented lab authority.","Close the loop with the provider either way; unexplained 'no' from the system is how clinicians learn to distrust and work around it."],
kn:"<p><strong>Beaker is laboratory science compiled into configuration:</strong> stability windows, container chemistry (which additive tube for which assay), minimum volumes, and add-on eligibility all mirror the lab's validated procedures — validated meaning experimentally established and regulatorily inspectable (CAP/CLIA), not preference. When the system rejects an add-on, it's usually the lab's science speaking through the build.</p><p>That makes the analyst a translator more often than a fixer on these tickets. Roughly half resolve as 'working as designed, and here's the clinical reason,' and delivering that answer with the actual rationale — rather than 'the system won't let you' — is what maintains clinician trust in the system's judgment. The other half are genuine build lag behind policy updates, and the discipline there is authority: specimen rules change on documented lab directorship approval, never on a persuasive phone call.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The convenience override:</strong> loosening a stability rule because redraws annoy providers — you've just authorized clinically unreliable results with your login attached.","<strong>The unexplained no:</strong> closing with 'working as designed' and no rationale — the provider learns nothing except that IT is unhelpful, and the ticket genre repeats forever.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"When the system says no, my job is to find out whether the build is wrong or the expectation is — and if it's the expectation, to deliver the clinical why, because 'working as designed' without a reason erodes trust in every future alert.\"</span>"]},

{p:2,m:"Radiant",t:"Finalized imaging study not visible in Epic",d:"Radiologist finalized a CT in PACS 45 minutes ago; the ordering provider sees the exam still 'in progress' and no report.",
tri:["One study or many? One = message-level defect; many = interface-level or a PACS-side change. Ask before tracing.","Is the accession number identical in Radiant and PACS for this exam? The join key is the first suspect in any matching failure.","Any PACS upgrades or dictation-system changes recently?"],
res:["Trace the return path: finalization in PACS/dictation emits a result/status message (ORU) keyed on the accession number, which updates exam status and delivers the report in Epic.","Check the results interface error queue for the stranded message — accession mismatches and unmapped status codes are the two standing causes.","If more studies are affected, engage the PACS administrator: a PACS-side status-code change strands <em>every</em> finalization until the mapping is updated.","Repair the mapping or message, confirm the report lands, and verify with the ordering provider — a finalized-but-invisible report is a delayed result with the same clinical weight as a delayed lab."],
kn:"<p><strong>Radiology runs as a two-system duet:</strong> Radiant owns orders, scheduling, and exam status; PACS owns the images; dictation produces the report. They synchronize over HL7 with the <strong>accession number as the join key</strong> — order out (ORM, populating the modality worklist so the tech's machine knows what to shoot), results and status back (ORU). Nearly every 'imaging is stuck' ticket is a failure somewhere on that round trip, and the accession number is where matching lives or dies.</p><p>The trap with the highest long-term cost is the manual shortcut: hand-updating the exam status in Epic to make the provider's screen look right, without repairing the underlying message. Now the status says final, the report still isn't coming (its delivery rides the same broken path), and the system's state has diverged from PACS's — a lie that compounds. Fix the message, let the status flow; the screen updating itself is your verification.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The cosmetic fix:</strong> manually flipping the status so the complaint stops — the report never arrives, the systems disagree, and the next analyst inherits a mystery.","<strong>Single-study tunnel vision:</strong> repairing one stranded message without checking whether a PACS change is stranding every finalization behind it.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"In multi-system workflows I never edit state by hand to match reality — I fix the message and let the state repair itself, because divergent state is a worse defect than a delayed one.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Ambulatory",t:"Results routing to the wrong In Basket pool",d:"Cardiology fellows' abnormal results are landing in a general pool nobody owns; two results sat unacknowledged for a day.",
tri:["Recalibrate urgency immediately: unowned abnormal results are a patient-safety exposure regardless of the P3 label. The two stranded results are today's problem; the routing rule is this week's.","What decides the target pool — ordering-provider attributes, service, pool membership rules — and did the fellowship's <code>SER</code> records change with the academic-year rotation?","How long has this been happening, and how many messages are stranded in total?"],
res:["Sweep first: search the general pool for every stranded result, get them acknowledged by the right clinicians <em>today</em>, and report the exposure window to the clinic manager — safety debt before technical debt.","Trace the routing chain: result message → ordering/authorizing provider → In Basket routing rules → pool membership. Fellows' SER records built at rotation time frequently miss the pool association or carry the wrong service line.","Fix the membership/routing rule and test with a live-fired result message, not an assumption.","Propose the durable control: a scheduled report on unowned/aging result pools, so the next routing break is detected by monitoring instead of by two results aging for a day."],
kn:"<p><strong>In Basket is where results acquire an owner — and unowned results are how patients fall through cracks.</strong> Routing rules translate 'this result exists' into 'this specific human is accountable for acting on it'; pools add coverage and escalation semantics on top. A misroute doesn't delete a result — it strands it where no one's workflow looks, which is functionally worse, because everything appears normal while an abnormal finding ages.</p><p>The pattern generalizes into one of the most important habits in clinical systems work: <strong>when you fix a routing rule, you have only fixed the future.</strong> The past — every message that already misrouted — must be swept and remediated explicitly. Closed-loop results management (every result acknowledged by an accountable clinician) is a recurring root-cause theme in real missed-diagnosis malpractice cases, which is why 'message routing' tickets deserve safety-ticket reflexes and why proposing aging-pool monitoring turns a fix into a control.</p>",
pit:["<strong>Fixing the future, abandoning the past:</strong> correcting the rule and closing without sweeping the stranded messages — the two unacknowledged results are still unacknowledged.","<strong>Trusting the label:</strong> working this as a P3 config tweak because that's what the ticket says, when the description contains the phrase 'sat unacknowledged for a day.'","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Routing fixes come in pairs — repair the rule for the future, sweep the strand for the past — and I read urgency from the clinical content of a ticket, not its priority field.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"MyChart",t:"Patient can't see yesterday's biopsy result in MyChart",d:"Patient portal message: labs from yesterday visible, pathology absent. Clinic asks if release is broken.",
tri:["Failure or configured delay? Pathology may carry a short, policy-approved release delay while routine labs release instantly — check config before declaring a defect.","Is the result actually finalized? Preliminary path results may be outside release scope entirely.","If a delay exists: is it a documented org policy with a compliance basis, or legacy config nobody has reviewed since the Cures rules?"],
res:["Check the result-release configuration for pathology result types: release timing, any delay window, and the category mapping that decides which rule applies.","If within a configured, policy-backed window: working as designed — arm the clinic with the exact policy and timing so they can answer the patient accurately instead of guessing.","If final, past any window, and unreleased: trace the category mapping — a new pathology result type that never got categorized falls through release rules silently.","Either way, capture the answer somewhere front-line staff can find it; this exact question arrives weekly forever."],
kn:"<p><strong>The 21st Century Cures Act's information-blocking rules inverted the default:</strong> results release to patients <em>immediately</em> unless a specific regulatory exception applies (chiefly preventing-harm, invoked case-by-case). The era of blanket 72-hour holds 'so the doctor calls first' is legally over — organizations that keep broad delays carry compliance risk, and release configuration is therefore policy-critical build that analysts change only with compliance/HIM authority behind them.</p><p>The second-order effect is cultural, and analysts sit in the middle of it: patients now routinely read a malignant pathology report before their physician has seen it. The mitigations are workflow, not config — pre-result counseling ('here's what you might see in the portal and when'), result-note templates written for patient eyes, and clinic processes for rapid outreach on serious findings. Expect the mirror-image ticket ('patient saw a scary result before we called — make it stop') and know that the correct answer is workflow support, not a quiet delay someone asks you to build.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The helpful illegal favor:</strong> adding a delay because a clinic asked — blanket delays without a documented exception are information blocking, a federal compliance issue with your build notes attached.","<strong>The confident wrong answer:</strong> telling the patient-facing team 'it will appear in 72 hours' based on how it used to work — always read the current config before quoting timing.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Result-release config is compliance-critical since Cures — the default is immediate, exceptions are narrow and documented, and I never touch release timing without HIM/compliance in the loop.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"MyChart",t:"Online scheduling books the wrong visit type",d:"New-patient cardiology requests through MyChart are landing as 15-minute follow-ups; front desk is manually rebooking each one.",
tri:["Walk the patient-facing decision tree yourself in the test portal — experiencing the misroute beats reading about it.","When did it start, and what changed then — a tree edit, a new visit type, a template rebuild?","How many wrong bookings already exist on future schedules? The cleanup is a workstream of its own."],
res:["Reproduce as a patient in the MyChart test environment: step through the scheduling questionnaire and watch which branch emits the wrong visit type.","Isolate the defect — typically one edited node, an inverted new-patient flag, or a new visit type mapped to the wrong template slots.","Fix the mapping, then <strong>regression-test every branch of the tree</strong>, not just the reported path — trees fail sideways, and the branch you didn't test is next week's ticket.","Quantify and hand off the cleanup: pull the wrongly booked future visits so the clinic can proactively correct them before patients show up to 15-minute slots needing 60."],
kn:"<p><strong>Patient self-scheduling is a three-part machine: decision trees (patient answers → visit type), visit types (defining duration and requirements), and provider templates (defining which slots accept which types).</strong> All three must agree for a booking to land right, and a defect in any one produces the same patient-visible symptom. The tree is the piece patients touch, so it gets the blame, but the analyst has to check all three joints.</p><p>What makes these defects expensive is silent scale: one inverted branch misbooks every patient who walks that path, and each misbook is a real human arriving to a slot that can't hold their visit — plus a physician's clinic day wrecked in 15-minute increments. Hence the two disciplines: full-branch regression testing after <em>any</em> tree edit (the matrix is finite; test it), and treating already-booked wrong visits as part of the incident, not someone else's cleanup. Self-service workflows concentrate risk precisely because no staff member reviews each transaction.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The single-path test:</strong> verifying the reported branch and shipping — decision trees fail on the paths nobody complained about yet.","<strong>Orphaning the cleanup:</strong> fixing the tree while dozens of wrong bookings sit on future schedules, each one a guaranteed bad clinic day.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Self-service workflows get full-matrix regression from me after any edit — no staff member reviews each transaction, so the build itself is the only quality gate there is.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Cupid",t:"Echo measurements not populating the structured report",d:"Sonographer measurements from the cart appear in the vendor system but the Cupid report template shows blanks.",
tri:["All measurements or specific fields? Specific = code mapping; all = interface or connectivity. The blank pattern is the diagnosis.","New cart, new cart software version, or new report template recently? Measurement code sets move with vendor software versions.","Which study types — one protocol or all echoes?"],
res:["Trace the structured-data path: cart → vendor cardiology system → interface → Cupid discrete report fields, with each measurement identified by a structured code (DICOM SR lineage).","Capture and diff a message from a working cart against the failing one — changed measurement codes light up immediately in a side-by-side.","Update the field mapping for the changed codes in the interface/Cupid build.","Validate against a <em>real study with the reading cardiologist</em> before closing — only a clinician confirms that LVEF landed in the LVEF field with a plausible value. Mapping without clinical validation is guessing with confidence."],
kn:"<p><strong>Cardiology device data moves as structured codes, not prose:</strong> the echo cart encodes every measurement (chamber dimensions, ejection fraction, valve gradients) as DICOM SR-style coded values, and Cupid's report templates map those codes to discrete fields. Discrete is the point — structured measurements feed registries, research datasets, and trending across studies, which free-text reports can't. A mapping break therefore isn't cosmetic; it's severing the data supply to everything downstream of the report.</p><p>The operational lesson is that <strong>vendor software versions are interface changes in disguise:</strong> a cart update can rename or restructure measurement codes without anyone considering it an 'integration change,' because it shipped as a device update. The mature control is including code-set regression in the acceptance testing for any cardiology device software change — and the validation rule is absolute: a clinician who reads these studies confirms the mapping, because only they can spot an EF value landing in the wrong field. (Domain bonus: this is the module where cardiovascular nursing documentation lives — Andy has a subject-matter expert at home.)</p>",
pit:["<strong>Confident guessing:</strong> remapping codes by name similarity without clinical validation — 'looks like the EF field' is how a gradient ends up trending as a dimension for six months.","<strong>Missing the version story:</strong> fixing the mapping without flagging that cart software updates bypassed integration review — the next update breaks it again.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Device software versions are interface changes wearing a different label — I push for code-set regression testing in device update acceptance, and no structured-data mapping ships without a clinician validating a real study.\"</span>"]},

{p:1,m:"Interfaces",t:"ADT feed lagging — downstream systems showing wrong beds",d:"Bed board and downstream ancillary systems show patients in prior locations; transport is being sent to wrong rooms. Queue latency climbing.",
tri:["P1 posture: wrong-location data misdirects care physically — transport, procedures, emergency response. Communicate impact to clinical coordination while diagnosing, not after.","Down, erroring, or slow? Latency climbing with no errors = throughput/consumer problem, a different animal from a failing interface.","Did volume spike — a mass-transfer event, a system backfill, a replay someone started?"],
res:["Check queue depth and consumption rate on the ADT outbound path: a stalled downstream consumer or a volume surge (bulk update replay is a classic self-inflicted one) are the standing causes.","Protect message ORDER above all: ADT events must process in sequence — a transfer applied before its admit corrupts downstream state in ways that outlast the incident.","If one downstream consumer is the bottleneck, engage that system's owner directly; resist any 'skip the queue' or purge suggestion, however senior the voice suggesting it.","After throughput recovers, verify reality: spot-check current census and locations against actual beds with nursing coordination — the queue reaching zero proves delivery, not correctness."],
kn:"<p><strong>ADT is the heartbeat interface of the hospital:</strong> A01 admit, A02 transfer, A03 discharge, A04 registration, A08 update — a real-time stream of who-is-where that every downstream system (lab, radiology, pharmacy, bed board, transport, billing) consumes to maintain its own picture of the hospital. When ADT lags, every one of those systems is confidently wrong in unison, and the failure is physical: a transporter at the wrong door, a portable X-ray at an empty bed.</p><p>The engineering principle that separates analysts from restart-button operators here: <strong>sequence integrity beats speed.</strong> ADT semantics are stateful — each event mutates the last known state — so processing out of order (or skipping 'stale' messages to catch up) creates corruption that persists after the queue drains, requiring manual reconciliation across multiple systems. Slow-but-ordered recovers by itself; fast-but-scrambled becomes next week's mystery tickets. Parallel processing and purging are exactly the interventions that feel productive and cause the second, worse incident.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The catch-up catastrophe:</strong> parallelizing or purging to drain the queue faster — you convert a latency incident into a state-corruption incident across five downstream systems.","<strong>Queue-zero complacency:</strong> declaring victory when depth hits zero without verifying census against physical reality — delivery isn't correctness.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"ADT is stateful, so my recovery rule is sequence over speed — slow and ordered self-heals; fast and scrambled corrupts every consumer. And recovery ends with a reality check against actual beds, not a queue metric.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Interfaces",t:"External vendor requests a new HL7 feed 'by Friday'",d:"A department bought a niche clinical software tool and the vendor emails asking for an ADT + results feed this week.",
tri:["Is there an approved project, a signed BAA, and a completed security review behind this purchase — or is this shadow IT surfacing at the integration layer?","What minimum data does the tool actually need to function? Vendors request everything by default; requirements analysis is your leverage.","Who in the department owns this purchase — the conversation about process needs a name, not a vendor."],
res:["Slow it down <em>correctly</em>: a new outbound PHI feed requires security review, a BAA, architecture review, and data-minimization scoping. 'By Friday' is the vendor's timeline, not the org's obligations.","Route to the formal integration intake process, and loop in the department leader personally — transparency converts 'IT is blocking us' into 'IT is protecting us,' and the difference is entirely in how you communicate it.","When approved, scope the feed to minimum-necessary fields and events; push back on the everything-request with specific questions about what each field drives in their product.","Document the request trail thoroughly — shadow-IT purchases surfacing as urgent integration demands is a recurring audit finding, and your paper trail is the org's defense."],
kn:"<p><strong>Every interface is a standing PHI disclosure channel, and HIPAA's minimum-necessary principle applies to system feeds exactly as it does to human access.</strong> An ADT feed carrying full demographics, coverage, and diagnoses to a tool that functions on MRN + location + timestamps is structural over-disclosure — repeated automatically, at volume, forever. Data minimization at feed design time is the single highest-leverage privacy decision in the integration lifecycle, because retrofitting a narrower feed after go-live essentially never happens.</p><p>The intake gauntlet exists for reasons that each map to a real historical failure: the BAA establishes the vendor's legal accountability for the PHI; security review evaluates their transport, storage, and breach posture; architecture review prevents the accumulation of undocumented point-to-point spaghetti that makes every future upgrade a minefield. The analyst's diplomatic craft is being the person who says 'yes, through the process' with a credible timeline — firm on the gate, generous with the map. This ticket is also a preview of security-architecture work: evaluating third-party data flows is a core function of the role at the end of Andy's roadmap.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The temporary feed that lives forever:</strong> standing up a 'quick interim' feed to be compliant later — interim integrations are permanent the moment clinical workflow depends on them.","<strong>Becoming the department of no:</strong> citing process without offering a path and a timeline — the department escalates over your head, and now the process itself is under attack.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"I apply minimum-necessary to interfaces the way IAM applies least-privilege to accounts — scope at design time, because nobody ever narrows a feed after go-live. And my 'no' always ships with a path and a date.\"</span>"]},

{p:2,m:"Interfaces",t:"Results interface error rate spiking since overnight",d:"Monitoring shows the lab results inbound feed erroring at 90%+ since ~05:00; a few units have started calling.",
tri:["Anchor the exact start time, then hunt for what changed at that moment — LIS maintenance, an upgrade, a courier migration. Interfaces rarely break spontaneously; they break when something upstream moves.","Read actual failed messages before theorizing: the filing error text names the failure mode, and 1,300 errors with one common denominator is one defect, not 1,300.","What's the clinical exposure right now — are criticals being called by phone per the lab's backup protocol?"],
res:["Pull the error queue and read a sample of failing messages; identify the shared cause (identifier format change and code-mapping gaps are the perennial winners).","Correlate the failure start against change records — ServiceNow CHGs, vendor maintenance windows, anything touching the sender.","Fix forward under emergency change: update the translation/mapping, validate in TST against replayed real messages, promote, then <strong>reprocess the deferred queue — never purge it</strong>. Every deferred message is a patient's result.","Close the clinical loop post-recovery: delayed-critical-results review with lab and nursing leadership, then RCA on the change-notification gap that let an upstream change land unannounced."],
kn:"<p><strong>The interface incident playbook, in order:</strong> scope via monitoring (when did it start, what's the rate) → read the error text (the message tells you why it failed) → correlate with changes (something moved at that timestamp) → fix forward under emergency change with TST validation → reprocess deferred messages → clinical safety review. Each step exists because skipping it has a named failure mode: skip the error text and you restart things randomly; skip TST and your fix becomes the second incident; skip reprocessing and results vanish; skip the safety review and 'resolved' papers over unexamined patient exposure.</p><p><strong>Deferred vs purged is the moral distinction of interface operations:</strong> deferred messages are recoverable patient data waiting for a fix; purging is the irreversible destruction of clinical results, and no queue-depth graph justifies it. The complementary lesson is detection: an interface erroring at 90% for two hours before a human noticed means monitoring thresholds and alerting are the real RCA finding — error-rate alarms should page the analyst before the nurse calls the help desk. (The full P1 version of this scenario is the companion incident simulation.)</p>",
pit:["<strong>The restart reflex:</strong> bouncing the interface before reading a single error message — it destroys diagnostic state and fixes nothing when the failure is in filing logic.","<strong>Technical closure, clinical omission:</strong> queue at zero, ticket closed, no delayed-criticals review — the IT question answered, the patient-safety question never asked.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"My interface incident order is fixed: read errors, correlate changes, fix forward, reprocess, then the safety review — and I treat 'purge the queue' suggestions as the destruction of patient results, because that's literally what they are.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Reporting",t:"Reporting Workbench report times out for ops director",d:"A director's daily census-detail report started timing out after she widened it to a 12-month lookback.",
tri:["What changed is stated: the lookback widened. This is tool-fit, not defect — the diagnosis is done; the work is the conversation.","Separate her two actual needs: operational (today's census, real-time) and analytical (12-month trend, historical). One report was serving both badly.","How does she consume it — glance, export, distribute? Shapes which analytics vehicle fits."],
res:["Explain the boundary honestly: Reporting Workbench queries the <em>live production database</em> in real time — brilliant for 'right now,' structurally wrong for 12-month scans, and the timeout is the system protecting production performance.","Split the need: restore her RWB report to the operational range (her daily workflow works again today), and rebuild the historical view in the analytics layer — Slicer Dicer for self-service exploration, or a Clarity/Caboodle report for a fixed monthly product.","Offer the 15-minute Slicer Dicer walkthrough — converting a repeat requester into a self-server is the highest-ROI teaching moment in reporting.","Note the pattern for the team: RWB timeout tickets are tool-fit tickets ~80% of the time; a saved explanation template pays for itself monthly."],
kn:"<p><strong>The Epic data stack, which every analyst should recite in their sleep:</strong> Chronicles (the live hierarchical production DB — fast for operational lookups, punished by broad historical scans) → Clarity (relational SQL Server, populated by nightly ETL — the workhorse for complex historical reporting, always ~a day behind) → Caboodle (dimensional warehouse on top — star schemas built for analytics and trending) → Slicer Dicer (self-service population exploration riding Caboodle). Reporting Workbench queries Chronicles directly, which defines both its superpower (real-time truth) and its hard boundary (narrow scope, short lookbacks).</p><p>The routing heuristic: <strong>real-time + operational + narrow = RWB; historical + broad + analytical = Clarity/Caboodle/Slicer Dicer.</strong> Half of all 'reporting is broken' tickets are actually 'right question, wrong tool,' which means the analyst's most valuable reporting skill isn't SQL — it's teaching the stack in two sentences so requesters route themselves. The nightly-ETL lag is the other recurring gotcha: 'why doesn't Clarity show this morning's patients' has the same answer every time, and a good analyst has it saved.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The brute-force accommodation:</strong> raising timeouts or scheduling the giant RWB query overnight — you're hammering the production database to avoid a 10-minute conversation about tools.","<strong>Fixing without teaching:</strong> quietly rebuilding it in Clarity yourself — she's served, but next month she widens another RWB report, because the mental model never transferred.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"I can draw the Epic data stack — Chronicles to Clarity to Caboodle to Slicer Dicer — and route any request to the right layer in one question: real-time operational or historical analytical? Most 'broken reports' are just misrouted questions.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Reporting",t:"Two dashboards disagree on the same readmission metric",d:"Quality's dashboard says 12.4%, the service line's says 9.8% for the same month; leadership wants to know which is 'right.'",
tri:["Get both metric definitions <em>in writing</em> before opening any query: inclusion/exclusion criteria, index-event logic, denominator population, data source, refresh timing.","Same source layer (Caboodle vs Clarity vs a manual extract someone maintains in Excel)? Different sources with different refresh times disagree legitimately.","Which definition does leadership actually intend when they say 'readmission rate' — and does anyone own that decision?"],
res:["Diff the definitions line by line: readmission metrics diverge on planned-readmission exclusions, observation-stay handling, transfer logic, and 30-day windowing — essentially never on 'the data is wrong.'","Trace each dashboard's lineage to its source table and refresh schedule; document both paths.","Reframe the deliverable for leadership: both numbers are 'right' <em>per their own definitions</em> — the actual problem is that the org has two definitions and no endorsed one. Route that decision to the data governance owner with your reconciliation as the evidence.","Embed the endorsed definition in the dashboard documentation itself once decided — undocumented metric logic is how this ticket regenerates every quarter."],
kn:"<p><strong>Metric fights are definition fights wearing a data costume.</strong> In healthcare analytics, ordinary words — readmission, mortality, census, length-of-stay — hide layered inclusion logic: does a planned chemo readmission count? An observation stay? A transfer from an outside hospital? CMS's regulatory definitions differ from internal quality's, which differ from the service line's operational view, and each is defensible for its purpose. Two correct implementations of two reasonable definitions will disagree forever, and no amount of SQL fixes that.</p><p>The analyst's real deliverable is <strong>reconciliation plus escalation to governance:</strong> show precisely where the definitions diverge, quantify each divergence's contribution to the gap, and hand the 'which one is ours' decision to the data governance function — because endorsing a definition is an organizational authority act, not a technical one. Data lineage documentation (this number ← this table ← this ETL ← this source) is the supporting discipline; dashboards without documented lineage are unfalsifiable, and unfalsifiable numbers eventually lose leadership's trust entirely.</p>",
pit:["<strong>Picking a winner:</strong> declaring one dashboard 'wrong' publicly — you've made an enemy of its owner and exceeded your authority in one move; the divergence analysis was your job, the endorsement wasn't.","<strong>The quiet patch:</strong> tweaking one dashboard to match the other so the question goes away — now two dashboards agree on a definition nobody governed.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"When numbers disagree I audit definitions before data — readmission metrics diverge on exclusions and windowing, almost never on wrong rows — and I deliver a reconciliation to governance rather than a verdict, because endorsing a definition is their authority, not mine.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Peripherals",t:"After-visit summaries printing at the old clinic location",d:"A clinic moved buildings last weekend; AVS printouts route to printers in the old suite.",
tri:["Printing succeeds — routing is wrong. Mapping problem, and specifically: were workstation records updated for the move, or did the machines just physically travel?","Which document types are affected? AVS, requisitions, school notes, and labels can each map to different queues — 'printing is fixed' requires enumerating them.","Is patient information currently printing unattended in the old suite? That's a privacy exposure, not just an inconvenience — check today."],
res:["Address the exposure first: confirm whether AVS output (which is PHI) is accumulating at unattended printers in the vacated space; retrieve/secure anything that has.","Check EPS routing for the moved workstations: workstation + department + document type resolve to queues, and physically relocated machines keep their old mappings until someone updates the records.","Update workstation-to-printer mappings for the new suite, coordinating with the field/desktop team on physical printer identities — the exact handshake Andy has run from the other side.","Test <em>every document type the clinic prints</em>, not just AVS, and contribute the finding forward: a pre-move checklist (workstation records, printer mappings, department addresses, lab routing) that turns the next clinic move from a ticket generator into a non-event."],
kn:"<p><strong>Clinic moves are configuration migrations that happen to involve furniture.</strong> Epic's print routing lives entirely in records — workstation identity, department association, EPS queue mappings — so relocating hardware without updating configuration strands every mapped output at the old address. The same principle extends beyond printing: department addresses on requisitions, lab specimen routing, even patient-facing directions in MyChart all reference build that a physical move silently invalidates.</p><p>Two deeper points ride along. First, the privacy angle is real and commonly missed: AVS documents printing unattended in a vacated suite is PHI exposure, and 'we moved' doesn't suspend HIPAA — the analyst who thinks to ask 'where is the output physically going right now' catches it. Second, the systemic fix is a process artifact, not build: a clinic-move checklist owned jointly by facilities, desktop, and the Epic team converts a predictable multi-ticket event into a checklist run — and proposing it is exactly the kind of cross-team process contribution that gets an analyst noticed beyond their queue.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The whack-a-mole week:</strong> fixing printers one complaint at a time instead of auditing the whole suite's mappings on ticket one — the clinic files five tickets and remembers all five.","<strong>Forgetting the paper is PHI:</strong> treating misrouted AVS purely as a routing nuisance while patient summaries pile up at an unattended printer in an empty suite.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Having worked the field side, I know a clinic move is a config migration — my instinct is to audit every mapping the move invalidated on day one, check where PHI is physically landing, and leave behind a move checklist so the next one is a non-event.\"</span>"]},

{p:2,m:"Downtime",t:"BCA downtime computer found dead during rounding",d:"Monthly check finds a unit's business-continuity PC powered off with a stale patient data snapshot from three weeks ago.",
tri:["Reframe urgency: no active outage, but if Epic had gone down last night this unit would have had <em>zero</em> current downtime data. A dead parachute is urgent on sunny days.","One machine, or is the snapshot job failing more broadly? One dead BCA is often the visible tip of a monitoring gap across the fleet.","Why three weeks undetected — is freshness checked only by monthly human rounding?"],
res:["Restore the machine (power, network, service), confirm the scheduled snapshot job executes, and verify <em>current</em> data lands — a running PC with a stale snapshot is the same failure with better optics.","Audit snapshot freshness across the entire BCA fleet today, not just this unit — assume the detection gap, not the device, is the real defect until proven otherwise.","Report the exposure window honestly: three weeks during which this unit's downtime fallback was fiction.","Propose the durable fix: automated freshness alerting (snapshot age > threshold → alert), replacing monthly human checks as the primary detection — humans verify; monitors detect."],
kn:"<p><strong>Downtime readiness is a layered system, and each layer covers a different failure mode:</strong> BCA (business continuity access) PCs hold locally stored snapshots of critical clinical data — census, meds, allergies, recent results — for when Epic is fully unreachable; SRO (shadow read-only) provides a live read-only Epic copy during production outages where infrastructure survives; paper forms are the final layer beneath both. Nurses and downtime procedures assume these layers exist and are current — an assumption someone has to make true continuously.</p><p>The principle that elevates this from a desktop ticket to an infrastructure insight: <strong>disaster infrastructure only proves itself during the disaster, so its health must be monitored like production — because during the outage, it <em>is</em> production.</strong> A stale parachute equals no parachute, and discovering it mid-jump is the one unrecoverable timing. The corollary is that detection design matters more than any single fix: monthly human rounding found this three weeks late; an automated freshness alarm finds it in an hour. Downtime drills — actually practicing on BCA data before it's needed — are the companion discipline, and units that drill perform measurably better in real outages.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The scope shrug:</strong> rebooting the one PC and closing — if the snapshot job or its monitoring is broken fleet-wide, you fixed one parachute in a plane full of dead ones.","<strong>The desktop-ticket framing:</strong> routing it as a simple hardware issue without surfacing the three-week detection gap — the gap is the finding; the dead PC is just its symptom.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"I hold downtime infrastructure to production monitoring standards — during the outage it IS production — and my fix for a stale BCA is never just the reboot; it's the freshness alerting that makes three-week-stale impossible to miss again.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Change",t:"Regression script fails in TST after the quarterly upgrade",d:"During SU testing, a medication workflow script that passed last quarter now errors at the verification step.",
tri:["Three suspects, three owners: a true Epic defect (vendor), local config colliding with the new version (you), or an outdated test script (also you). The triage IS deciding which.","Check Nova release notes for this workflow area <em>first</em> — a documented behavior change means the system is right and the script is stale.","Is the failure reproducible cleanly, with exact steps? Sherlock submissions without clean repro steps bounce and burn days."],
res:["Read the targeted Nova notes for the affected area before touching anything — many 'failures' are intended changes the release notes announced and the script didn't absorb.","If Nova documents the change: update the regression script, note the behavior change for end-user communication if it's visible, move on.","If genuinely broken and undocumented: build a clean reproduction (steps, screenshots, environment), then log via <code>Sherlock</code> — quality of repro determines vendor response speed more than severity settings do.","If local config assumed the old behavior: fix the build in TST and add it to the upgrade change package so PRD gets it with the SU. Either way, the script gets updated — regression libraries are living documents or they're theater."],
kn:"<p><strong>The quarterly upgrade (SU) machine has three instruments:</strong> Nova release notes tell you what Epic changed (reviewed by area ownership before testing starts, not after failures appear); regression script libraries prove your critical workflows survived; Sherlock is the defect channel to the vendor, where reproduction quality is the currency. The core triage skill is attribution — 'Epic broke it' vs 'Epic changed it' vs 'our config assumed the old behavior' — because each has a different owner, timeline, and fix, and misattributing wastes everyone's cycle.</p><p>The maturity marker is treating regression scripts as maintained assets: a script library that nobody updates after documented behavior changes generates false alarms every quarter until testers start ignoring failures — the testing equivalent of alert fatigue, with the same endgame of a real defect sailing through. Organizations run SU testing on a rhythm (notes review → targeted testing → fix window → CAB → promote), and analysts who arrive already fluent in that rhythm — rather than treating each quarter as a surprise — are the ones trusted with the riskier application areas.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The premature Sherlock:</strong> logging a vendor defect before reading Nova — the bounce ('working as designed, see note 4471') costs days and credibility.","<strong>The stale script spiral:</strong> leaving the script un-updated after a documented change — it fails again next quarter, someone marks it 'known failure,' and the script library starts rotting from that square.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"My SU triage order is fixed: Nova first, then attribution — vendor defect, vendor change, or our config — because each has a different owner. And I treat regression scripts like production code: a failing test gets fixed or updated, never ignored.\"</span>"]},

{p:2,m:"Change",t:"Data Courier migration overwrote a local record",d:"After last night's courier run, a department's carefully tuned workqueue rules reverted; the source environment had an older version of the same record.",
tri:["This is a dual-edit collision: reconstruct who edited the record in which environment, when — the timeline is the diagnosis.","Is the lost configuration recoverable from record audit/history, or does it need rebuilding from the department's memory?","Was the PRD-direct edit an emergency fix that never got backfilled to lower environments? That's the standing pattern."],
res:["Reconstruct the sequence: the record was edited directly in PRD (probably a legitimate quick fix weeks ago) while an older version of the same record rode last night's courier package from TST — the migration won, silently.","Restore the intended configuration from record audit history; verify function with the department before closing — restored config that nobody validates is Schrödinger's fix.","Repair the process, not just the record: the emergency PRD edit should have been backfilled to POC/TST at the time, and the courier package should have been conflict-reviewed before send.","Raise it in the team's change review as a pattern, not an accusation — courier discipline failures are systemic and repeat until the workflow changes; naming individuals just drives the next emergency edit underground."],
kn:"<p><strong>Data Courier is Epic's deployment pipeline — POC → TST → PRD — and its cardinal sin is environment drift:</strong> editing production directly while a different version of the same record moves through the pipeline guarantees a future silent overwrite. The analogy that lands instantly with anyone from software: <strong>it's a merge conflict without a merge tool.</strong> Git would block the push and show you the diff; Courier just ships, and last-writer-wins with no diff view. Therefore the process — conflict review before send, no undocumented PRD edits, immediate backfill of any emergency production change to lower environments — <em>is</em> the merge tool, and it only works if everyone runs it.</p><p>The reason emergency PRD edits keep happening is that they're sometimes correct: a P1 at 2 AM doesn't wait for a POC→TST→PRD cycle. The discipline isn't 'never touch PRD' — it's that every emergency edit creates a synchronization debt that must be paid immediately (backfill to lower environments, document in the change record) or it becomes a landmine with a random fuse. Teams that track sync debt explicitly stop having courier surprises; teams that rely on memory have one per quarter, forever.</p>",
pit:["<strong>Blaming the tool:</strong> 'Courier overwrote it' — Courier did exactly what it was told; the un-backfilled emergency edit three weeks ago armed the landmine.","<strong>Restoring without validating:</strong> pulling old config from audit history and closing — the department tuned those rules for reasons; only they can confirm the restoration is complete.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"I explain Courier discipline as merge conflicts without a merge tool — every emergency PRD edit creates sync debt that must be backfilled same-day, because last-writer-wins with no diff means the process is the only protection there is.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Efficiency",t:"Physician requests help — 'Epic is eating my evenings'",d:"A PCP reports 90+ minutes of after-hours charting daily and asks if anything can be configured to help.",
tri:["Pull the provider-efficiency analytics (Signal) before proposing anything: where does the time actually go — note composition, In Basket volume, order entry? Prescription before diagnosis is malpractice in this domain too.","What personalization already exists — SmartPhrases, preference lists, filters — and is it stale?","Is the burden individual (personalization fixes it) or structural (message routing, refill volume, team process) — because tips can't fix a team problem?"],
res:["Review the Signal data with the physician: after-hours time, time-in-notes vs In Basket vs orders, comparison to specialty peers. The data usually surprises them — and it converts the session from generic tips to targeted intervention.","Run a personalization session against what the data showed: personal SmartPhrases/SmartLinks for their top note types, order preference lists for their top diagnoses, In Basket QuickActions and filters for their message patterns.","Where the data shows structural burden — disproportionate refill or result-message volume — recommend the team-level fixes (routing protocols, delegation) to the clinic manager; don't let a systemic problem be treated as a personal efficiency failure.","Follow up in 30 days <em>with the same metrics</em>: measured before/after is what makes this a program instead of a favor, and it's the evidence that funds the next session."],
kn:"<p><strong>'Pajama time' — after-hours EHR work — is one of the most-studied drivers of physician burnout, and burnout is a patient-safety variable, not an HR nicety:</strong> it correlates with error rates, attrition, and care quality. Epic ships the measurement layer (Signal): per-provider analytics on time-in-system, after-hours minutes, note composition patterns, In Basket load, all benchmarkable against specialty peers. This transforms efficiency work from vibes ('try these ten tips') into medicine ('your data shows 60% of after-hours time is In Basket; here's the targeted fix').</p><p>The strategic insight is that <strong>personalization sessions are among the highest-ROI activities in an analyst's portfolio</strong> — an hour that returns 30-45 minutes per day to a physician compounds over years — and that the individual/structural distinction is the ethical core of the work: if the data shows a physician drowning in refill requests that a protocol could delegate, coaching them to type faster is blaming the swimmer for the current. Analysts who bring data, fix what's personal, escalate what's structural, and measure the outcome become the people physicians request by name — which is worth more than any certification in the internal-mobility economy.</p>",
pit:["<strong>Tips without data:</strong> delivering the generic top-ten efficiency list — it lands as condescension when the actual problem is structural message volume no shortcut can fix.","<strong>The unmeasured favor:</strong> running the session and never re-checking the metrics — without before/after numbers, the program has no evidence, and the next physician's session is harder to justify.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"For provider efficiency I diagnose before prescribing — Signal data first, then targeted personalization, then a 30-day re-measure — and I'm careful to escalate structural burden as a team problem rather than coach individuals to survive a broken workflow.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Ambulatory",t:"E-visit questionnaire responses not attaching to encounters",d:"Patients complete symptom questionnaires via the portal but clinicians find empty encounters; staff re-ask everything by phone.",
tri:["Are responses captured-but-unlinked, or never captured? Locate the data first — the recovery plan depends entirely on whether it exists.","One questionnaire or all e-visit types? One = that questionnaire's config (likely a recent version); all = the e-visit workflow's linkage build.","Was the questionnaire edited/versioned recently? Versioning is the prime suspect in attach failures."],
res:["Hunt for the submitted data — in most cases it's captured and orphaned, not lost: a new questionnaire version broke the mapping to the e-visit encounter type while the old version's linkage kept the lights on until it aged out.","Re-link the current questionnaire version to the encounter workflow; test the <em>complete round trip</em> in the portal test environment — submit as a patient, verify arrival as a clinician. Both sides or it isn't tested.","Recover the orphaned responses: route or backfill them to their encounters where feasible — patients answered these questions in good faith, and re-asking by phone is the org quietly discarding their effort.","Add questionnaire linkage verification to the standard checklist for any questionnaire edit — versioning breaking the attach hop is a repeatable failure with a checkable fix."],
kn:"<p><strong>Patient-entered data runs its own pipeline: questionnaire build → portal presentation → response capture → encounter linkage → clinician visibility.</strong> The treacherous property of asynchronous patient workflows is that the two ends can disagree while both are telling the truth: the patient saw a success confirmation (capture worked), the clinician sees an empty encounter (linkage failed). Any workflow where the submitter and consumer are different people at different times must be tested end-to-end from both seats — the patient's and the clinician's — because no single vantage point can see the break.</p><p>Versioning is the recurring villain: editing a questionnaire creates a new version, and configuration that pointed at the old version (encounter linkage, scoring, routing) doesn't always follow automatically. The deeper duty is to the data itself — orphaned responses represent real patients' time and clinical information, and 'just re-ask by phone' both wastes that and delays care. Recovering orphans, not merely fixing the pipe, is the complete resolution; and the same fix-future/sweep-past pairing from In Basket routing applies here identically. Patterns repeat across modules — recognizing them is what makes the second year of this job faster than the first.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The rebuild reflex:</strong> deleting and recreating the questionnaire to 'start clean' — you've destroyed the version lineage and possibly stranded the orphaned responses permanently.","<strong>One-seat testing:</strong> verifying the fix from the clinician side only — the patient-side submission path is half the pipeline and fails independently.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Asynchronous patient workflows I test from both seats — submit as the patient, consume as the clinician — and my resolution standard includes recovering orphaned data, because patients' answers are clinical information, not disposable form entries.\"</span>"]},

{p:1,m:"ClinDoc",t:"Epic slow to frozen across multiple inpatient units",d:"Hyperspace sessions freezing intermittently house-wide for 20 minutes; help desk is flooded; no maintenance scheduled.",
tri:["This exceeds any single analyst by design — confirm severity and invoke the major-incident process <em>now</em>; the ticket queue is not the vehicle for a house-wide outage.","Gather pattern intelligence while escalating, not instead of it: all workflows or specific ones? All access paths (Citrix, local, mobile) or one? Intermittent-freeze fingerprints differ from hard-down fingerprints.","Is clinical degradation approaching downtime-procedure territory? That call needs prep before it needs making."],
res:["Declare or join the major incident bridge: infrastructure, Epic Technical Services (via Sherlock), Citrix, network, and database teams converge — the CSA's role there is <strong>clinical impact intelligence</strong>, not out-debugging the infrastructure engineers.","Feed the bridge structured impact data: which workflows degrade, which units, what still works, workaround status — 'medication administration functional, results review intermittent' is worth more to the bridge than another person guessing at root cause.","Stage downtime readiness with nursing supervisors before it's needed: BCA freshness, SRO availability, paper forms — if degradation deepens, the transition should be a decision, not a scramble.","Own clinical communication in plain language throughout: what's degraded, what still works, what to do meanwhile — and afterward, the analyst's RCA contribution is the clinical-impact timeline, which only you were positioned to capture."],
kn:"<p><strong>In a major incident, the analyst's highest function is translation:</strong> infrastructure teams speak in servers, sessions, and packet loss; the bridge needs to hear 'med administration is safe, results review is degraded, use downtime forms if X worsens.' Severity in healthcare IT is defined by <em>clinical impact</em>, not system metrics — a 10% session-failure rate reads as minor in infrastructure terms and means one in ten medication passes is fighting the system. The person who converts between those two languages, in both directions, is load-bearing on every bridge.</p><p>Major Incident Management (MIM) exists because ad-hoc heroics fail at scale: defined severity levels, a bridge with a commander, structured comms cadence, and role clarity. Role clarity is the analyst's discipline — the pull toward debugging alongside infrastructure is strong and almost always wrong; your unique contribution (clinical impact mapping, workaround coordination, downtime staging, stakeholder comms) is the thing nobody else on the bridge can do. Practice the sentence that anchors it: <em>severity is defined by clinical impact, not system metrics</em> — it reframes every status update you'll ever give, and it's the sentence that makes clinical leadership trust IT during the worst hour of the quarter.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The rabbit hole:</strong> spending the incident debugging Citrix session logs while nobody maps clinical impact or stages downtime procedures — you've abandoned the one role only you could fill to understudy a role that was covered.","<strong>Comms in system-speak:</strong> telling nursing 'the XenApp farm is shedding sessions' — clinical stakeholders need what-still-works and what-to-do, in their language, on a cadence.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"On a major incident bridge my job is translation and clinical impact intelligence — infrastructure owns the root cause; I own knowing whether it's safe to keep administering meds, and making sure both sides hear each other in their own language.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Security",t:"Audit request: who accessed a specific patient's chart",d:"Privacy office asks for a 90-day access report on one patient record as part of a complaint investigation.",
tri:["Verify the request's authority first: chart-access audits run through the privacy office with a documented case — an informal 'can you check who looked at' from anyone else gets politely routed to privacy, no exceptions, no matter their title.","Clarify scope precisely: views only, or all touches — prints, exports, In Basket forwards, report inclusion? Investigations turn on completeness.","Confirm the secure delivery channel before running anything — the report itself will be sensitive."],
res:["Run the access log report for the record and window; include per-entry context: user, role, department, access type, and the workflow context of each touch — raw timestamps without context force privacy to come back with twenty follow-ups.","Deliver data, not judgment: do not flag entries as 'suspicious' or annotate with theories — investigation and determination are the privacy office's function, and your editorializing can taint their process.","Handle the report as the sensitive artifact it is: secure delivery per policy, no casual email attachments, chain of custody noted in the ticket.","Keep your own conduct audit-clean: access only what the documented request requires — you are generating evidence inside an investigation, and your access is being logged too."],
kn:"<p><strong>Every touch of every chart is logged — views, edits, prints, queries — and access auditing is a formal HIPAA function, not an IT favor.</strong> The audit log's completeness is the technical foundation under the entire accountability model: it's what makes break-the-glass workable (access now, review later), what investigates complaints, and what deters the snooping that celebrity and coworker charts otherwise attract. The analyst's craft is producing audit output that is complete, contextualized, and untainted — role and workflow context attached to each access, because 'nurse on the unit during the admission' and 'billing clerk from another campus at midnight' are the same log entry until context makes them different.</p><p>The governing discipline is <strong>role separation, and it protects everyone including you:</strong> the analyst produces accurate data; the privacy office investigates and determines; neither crosses over. Editorializing ('entry 14 looks suspicious') can bias or legally taint an investigation; conversely, quietly running access reports for a curious manager outside the documented process makes <em>you</em> the privacy incident. This ticket genre is also the clearest bridge in the entire queue to security-analyst work — same logs, same discipline, deeper analysis — which is precisely the next station on Andy's roadmap.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The helpful opinion:</strong> annotating the report with your suspicions — you may have just tainted an investigation and turned yourself into a witness.","<strong>The informal favor:</strong> running an access check for a manager who asked casually — without the privacy office's documented request, that lookup is itself a reportable access.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"On audit requests I hold role separation as a bright line — I produce complete, contextualized data through the documented process and leave determination to privacy, because in an investigation, the integrity of the evidence chain includes me.\"</span>"]},

{p:2,m:"HIM / Identity",t:"Two charts, one patient — possible duplicate MRN",d:"ICU nurse reports allergies she documented this morning have 'disappeared.' Investigation shows the patient has two MRNs — today's documentation is split across both records.",
tri:["Confirm it's truly one human with two records (demographics, DOB, SSN fragments match) — and not two humans sharing one record, which is a different and far worse animal.","What's split across the two records <em>today</em> — allergies, meds, orders? Active-care data split is an immediate safety exposure, not a data-quality chore.","Which record is the care team actively using right now?"],
res:["Immediate mitigation before any merge talk: make the care team aware of both records, designate which one is used for care <em>today</em>, and manually reconcile safety-critical data (allergies, med list) into it.","Submit both records to the HIM patient-identity team for duplicate evaluation and merge — <strong>analysts never merge records themselves</strong>; merges are HIM-governed and nearly irreversible.","After HIM completes the merge, verify consolidated data with the care team: allergies, meds, results, and documentation all present on the surviving record.","Feed the RCA: how was the duplicate created — registration search miss, name-spelling variant, DOB transposition? Registration search-before-create discipline is the upstream fix."],
kn:"<p><strong>Duplicates and overlays are opposite failures with opposite dangers.</strong> A duplicate is one human with two records: their truth is <em>split</em>, so clinicians act on incomplete information — the missing allergy is the classic harm. An overlay is two humans in one record: their truths are <em>mixed</em>, so clinicians act on someone else's information — wrong blood type, wrong history, and untangling it means forensically separating every data element. Duplicate = urgent; overlay = emergency.</p><p>The machinery behind this is the EMPI (enterprise master patient index) and its matching algorithms — probabilistic scoring on names, DOBs, identifiers — plus the human registration workflow that either searches properly before creating or doesn't. Merges are HIM's authority because they're high-consequence and effectively one-way: data provenance blurs after consolidation. The analyst's lane is detection, immediate clinical mitigation, evidence for HIM, and the upstream process fix — a lane worth naming explicitly, because 'can you just merge these' will be asked of you, and the correct answer is a fast referral, not a favor.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The forbidden favor:</strong> performing the merge yourself because HIM's queue is slow — if the 'duplicate' is actually an overlay, you've just commingled two patients' records under your own login.","<strong>The paperwork framing:</strong> routing to HIM and walking away while today's allergies and meds sit split across two active records — the merge can wait days; the clinical reconciliation cannot wait an hour.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Duplicates split one patient's truth; overlays mix two patients' truths — I treat the first as urgent, the second as an emergency, and neither as something an analyst merges alone.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"OpTime",t:"Preference card pulling wrong supplies for total knees",d:"OR calls: case carts for Dr. M's total knee cases keep arriving with wrong implant sizing trays and missing supplies. Staff are running mid-case for items — twice this week.",
tri:["One surgeon's card or the procedure's cards generally? One surgeon = his card is stale; all = the procedure build changed.","Did the surgeon's actual practice change (new implant system, new technique) while the card stayed frozen in the old world?","What's the waste and delay cost so far — opened-but-unused supplies and intra-op runner minutes are the numbers that get this prioritized."],
res:["Pull the preference card's edit history and compare against what the surgeon's team says he actually uses now — cards drift from practice silently, one substitution at a time.","Convene the ten-minute triangle: surgeon's team + OR materials/sterile processing + you. The card update needs clinical sign-off, not analyst guesswork about implant trays.","Update the card, then <strong>monitor the next two or three cases</strong> with the OR charge nurse before closing — a preference card fix is only proven by a correct cart arriving.","Surface the systemic gap: if this card rotted, others have too — propose a periodic card review cycle for high-volume procedures, with the waste dollars from this ticket as the justification."],
kn:"<p><strong>Preference cards are where clinical practice, supply chain, and money meet.</strong> A card drives what's picked for the case cart, what gets charged, and how the case is scheduled — so a stale card simultaneously creates clinical delay (runner leaves mid-case), waste (opened-unused supplies are discarded), and charge inaccuracy. OR time is among the most expensive real estate in the hospital, measured in tens of dollars per minute, which is why 'the cart was wrong twice' is a bigger ticket than it sounds.</p><p>The deeper pattern is <strong>configuration drift from practice:</strong> the card was right the day it was built, and every un-communicated change in the surgeon's technique since then widened the gap. That's the same disease as stale regression scripts and un-backfilled courier edits — build that encodes a moment in time, maintained by nobody. The cure is always a review cadence with a clinical owner, and the analyst who proposes it (with waste dollars attached) is doing systems work, not ticket work.</p>",
pit:["<strong>Guessing the medicine:</strong> updating implant trays on the card from the OR's verbal description without the surgeon's team confirming — you've now encoded a second wrong version with fresher timestamps.","<strong>Closing on the edit:</strong> the fix is proven by the next correct case cart, not by the commit — preference card tickets that close at build time have a habit of reopening mid-surgery.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Preference cards drift from practice silently — my fix is the update plus the review cadence, because a card is only correct on the day somebody last verified it.\"</span>"]},

{p:2,m:"ASAP (ED)",t:"ED track board frozen on the wall displays",d:"Charge nurse reports both hallway wall boards stopped refreshing 40 minutes ago. Workstation views are fine — staff are shoulder-surfing desktops to see the department.",
tri:["Workstations fine + wall boards frozen = the data is healthy; the <em>display endpoints</em> are the problem. That one contrast eliminates the entire ASAP build from suspicion.","What runs those boards — kiosk PCs with auto-login display accounts? When did their sessions start (timeout math)?","Any recent security policy push — session timeout, credential expiry — that would hit unattended accounts first?"],
res:["Confirm the mechanism: wall boards typically run kiosk workstations on dedicated display accounts, and the classic failure is an expired session or credential — often right after a security policy change that nobody exempted display accounts from.","Restart the kiosk sessions to restore the boards now; then fix the cause — display-account session policy, auto-login configuration, or the credential that silently expired.","Coordinate with the desktop team on the endpoint side (another two-sided ticket a former field tech reads instantly).","Propose a heartbeat monitor for display endpoints: a frozen track board should page somebody, not wait for a charge nurse to notice 40 minutes in."],
kn:"<p><strong>The ED track board is situational awareness, not decoration:</strong> who's waiting, who's deteriorating, who's been in a room too long, where the LWBS (left-without-being-seen) risk is building. When the boards freeze, the department falls back to fragmented workstation views and verbal updates — exactly the conditions where a long-waiting patient goes unnoticed. That's why a 'display issue' in the ED carries clinical weight that the same ticket in a conference room wouldn't.</p><p>The structural tension worth understanding: unattended clinical displays live at the collision point of <strong>security policy and clinical operations</strong>. Session timeouts, credential expiry, and auto-lock policies exist for excellent reasons — and applied unexempted to a kiosk account, they kill the track board on a schedule. The mature resolution is a governed exemption class for display endpoints (locked-down accounts, no interactive access, physical security compensating controls) plus monitoring — not an endless cycle of someone rebooting the board every time the policy fires.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The reboot treadmill:</strong> restarting the kiosk session and closing — the security policy that killed it will kill it again on the same timer next week.","<strong>Fighting the wrong battle:</strong> asking security to weaken the global timeout policy instead of proposing a governed display-account exemption class with compensating controls.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Unattended clinical displays sit where security policy collides with situational awareness — the fix is a governed exemption class plus heartbeat monitoring, not a weekly reboot ritual.\"</span>"]},

{p:2,m:"Grand Central",t:"Outside-hospital transfer request stuck 'pending' for six hours",d:"Transfer center accepted a patient from a community hospital this morning. The request never progressed — no bed assigned, sending facility calling repeatedly, patient still waiting there.",
tri:["Where exactly is the request stuck — which workflow status, and whose workqueue owns the next action? Stuck workflows always have an owner who didn't act or a routing that reached no one.","One request or a pattern? Check the whole transfer workqueue for aging items before assuming this is a one-off.","What's the patient's clinical status at the sending facility? Six hours matters differently for a stable ortho transfer vs a deteriorating one — urgency is clinical, as always."],
res:["Trace the request through its workflow states and find the gap — commonly a workqueue whose covering user is out, or a routing rule pointing at a role nobody currently holds.","Unstick this request now (reassign, progress the bed search with patient placement) and communicate directly with the transfer center and sending facility — they've been calling into silence for hours.","Sweep the queue: every other aging request in the same workqueue gets triaged today, because a broken queue rarely strands exactly one item.","Durable fix: aging alerts on transfer workqueues (any item pending beyond a threshold pages the owner's chain) plus coverage rules so a queue never depends on a single human's presence."],
kn:"<p><strong>Grand Central runs the hospital's logistics — bed planning, transfers, patient movement — as workflow, and workflow lives or dies on workqueue ownership.</strong> A workqueue is a promise that somebody watches it; an unwatched queue is where requests, and therefore patients, silently wait. Transfer requests are the highest-stakes version: a patient at a community hospital may be there precisely because they need a level of care only your facility provides, so every stuck hour is potential deterioration at a facility less equipped to handle it — with EMTALA-adjacent obligations hovering over the whole exchange.</p><p>The universal pattern this ticket teaches: <strong>every queue needs an owner, coverage, and an aging alarm.</strong> Any one of the three missing eventually produces this incident — the owner went on PTO (no coverage), the routing rule pointed at a vacated role (no owner), or items aged silently (no alarm). The same fix-future/sweep-past pairing applies here too: repairing the queue without triaging everything already stranded in it fixes tomorrow while abandoning today. Notice how often that pairing recurs across modules — In Basket, e-visits, now transfers — pattern recognition is the compounding asset of this job.</p>",
pit:["<strong>Rescuing one patient:</strong> unsticking the reported request while five others age in the same broken queue — the sweep is the job; the report was just the alarm.","<strong>Silent repair:</strong> fixing the routing without calling the transfer center and sending facility — they've spent six hours assuming your organization is ignoring them, and that relationship damage outlives the ticket.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Every workqueue needs an owner, coverage, and an aging alarm — an unwatched queue is where patients wait, and my fix always includes sweeping what already aged, not just repairing the routing.\"</span>"]},

{p:2,m:"Telehealth",t:"An afternoon of video visits failing to connect",d:"One clinic's telehealth session is melting down: patients stuck at 'waiting to join,' providers staring at empty video panels, schedulers asking whether to cancel the rest of the day.",
tri:["Workaround before diagnosis: can this afternoon convert to phone visits right now? The schedule is burning at 20-minute intervals while you debug.","One clinic or enterprise-wide? Enterprise = vendor platform outage; one clinic = that clinic's configuration, context launch, or device fleet.","What changed — vendor SDK update, certificate rotation, browser version push to the clinic's machines?"],
res:["Trigger the fallback first: schedulers convert the afternoon to phone visits per the telehealth downtime workflow — care continues and visits remain documentable while you work.","Check the vendor status page and your integration configuration in parallel: the join flow is Epic scheduling → context launch → vendor platform token handshake → video session, and each hop fails differently.","Isolate the hop (vendor logs and your integration logs usually name it — expired token config and vendor regional outages are the two front-runners), fix or wait out accordingly, then verify with a live test visit before telling the clinic it's safe.","Post-incident: make sure the phone-visit fallback is a documented, rehearsed workflow rather than something improvised today — the next outage is a vendor decision away."],
kn:"<p><strong>Telehealth is a chain of custody across two organizations:</strong> Epic owns scheduling and the context launch, the video vendor owns the platform and session, and a token handshake stitches them together. That means half the failure surface belongs to a company you can't log into — so triage starts with 'whose hop is failing' (their status page, your integration logs) rather than assuming the problem is yours to fix. Enterprise-wide failure points at the vendor; single-clinic failure points at your config or that clinic's endpoints.</p><p>The operational lesson is that <strong>graceful degradation is a workflow, not a feature:</strong> a video visit that converts to a phone visit in two minutes is a minor hiccup; one that dies while everyone debugs is a cancelled appointment, a rescheduling burden, and a patient who took the afternoon off work for nothing. Post-pandemic, telehealth is core clinical infrastructure — for some patients (rural, mobility-limited, behavioral health) it's the only practical access — so its downtime workflow deserves the same rehearsal discipline as any clinical downtime procedure.</p>",
pit:["<strong>Debugging while the schedule burns:</strong> spending 40 minutes in integration logs before anyone converts visits to phone — the workaround costs two minutes and saves the afternoon.","<strong>Blaming patient tech literacy:</strong> the reflexive 'they're probably clicking the wrong link' — when a whole session fails at once, the pattern is systemic, and that assumption just delays real triage.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Telehealth failures get the workaround first — convert to phone in minutes, keep care flowing — then I isolate which hop of the Epic-to-vendor chain broke, because half that chain belongs to a company I can't log into.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"API / FHIR",t:"SMART on FHIR app failing authentication since Monday",d:"The diabetes-management app embedded in Hyperspace won't launch — providers get an auth error. The vendor insists nothing changed on their end. It worked Friday.",
tri:["One app or all integrated apps? All = your API gateway/authorization server; one = that app's registration, credentials, or certificate.","What has a Monday-shaped expiry? Certificates and client secrets expire on schedules nobody remembers — 'it worked Friday' plus 'nothing changed' is the signature of a silent expiration.","Check your side's logs before accepting the vendor's 'nothing changed' — both sides always believe that, and one is always wrong."],
res:["Pull the authorization logs from the API layer (Interconnect-side): the token endpoint's rejection reason typically names the failure outright — expired client certificate or rotated-but-not-communicated secret being the perennial winners.","Coordinate the re-issuance with the vendor: new cert/secret exchanged through the proper registration process, not pasted through email.","Verify the full launch flow end-to-end from inside Hyperspace — authorize, token, FHIR API calls — before declaring it fixed.","The durable fix: a certificate-and-secret expiry calendar with alerts 30 days out, covering every integrated app. Expiry is a scheduled outage that nobody scheduled; the calendar turns it back into a scheduled event."],
kn:"<p><strong>SMART on FHIR is the standard that lets third-party apps launch inside the EHR with context:</strong> the app authorizes via OAuth (authorize endpoint → token endpoint), receives a scoped token, and calls FHIR APIs for exactly the data its scopes permit — the patient in context, not the whole database. Epic's web-service layer (Interconnect) fronts this. Understanding the flow means auth failures decompose cleanly: launch context, authorization, token issuance, or API access — four hops, four different fixes.</p><p>The recurring villain in this genre is <strong>credential lifecycle:</strong> certificates and client secrets are issued with expiry dates during an implementation everyone forgets, and they detonate months later on a quiet Monday. Both parties truthfully say 'nothing changed' — nothing did; something <em>expired</em>, which feels like nothing until it isn't. This ticket is also a quiet preview of API governance work: every integrated app is a scoped data channel with credentials to manage, scopes to review, and an inventory someone must own — which, on Andy's roadmap, is IAM analyst territory with a FHIR accent.</p>",
pit:["<strong>Accepting 'nothing changed':</strong> ping-ponging blame with the vendor for two days when your own token-endpoint logs named the expired cert in the first hour — read your logs before negotiating reality.","<strong>Fixing without the calendar:</strong> re-issuing the cert and closing — the same app, and every other app, will detonate on its own forgotten schedule.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Certificate expiry is a scheduled outage nobody scheduled — I keep an expiry calendar with 30-day alerts for every integrated app, because 'it worked Friday and nothing changed' is almost always something that expired.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Interfaces",t:"State immunization registry rejecting our submissions",d:"Public-health reporting dashboard shows VXU messages to the state immunization registry bouncing since Tuesday — hundreds of rejects and climbing. Nobody noticed for three days.",
tri:["Read the ACK rejection codes first — the state's acknowledgments name exactly what they now dislike about your messages.","What happened Tuesday on the <em>state's</em> side? Public-health endpoints upgrade on government timelines and announce it in newsletters nobody reads.","How deep is the reject backlog, and does the state expect resubmission of everything bounced?"],
res:["Pull the ACK errors: typically the state's upgrade began enforcing a newly required field or a newer code-set version (CVX/MVX vaccine codes being the classic), and your messages are now 'wrong' by rules that changed under you.","Update the mapping/message build to the state's new specification and validate against their test endpoint if one exists.","Resubmit the rejected backlog — three days of immunizations must reach the registry, not just future ones; verify acceptance ACKs on the resubmitted batch.","Close the detection gap: subscribe the team to the state's release notifications, and alert on reject-rate thresholds — three days of silent bouncing means nobody was watching the ACKs."],
kn:"<p><strong>Public-health interfaces (VXU for immunizations, ELR for lab reporting) have a property your internal interfaces don't: the other side changes on government timelines, with government communication.</strong> A state registry upgrade that tightens validation instantly makes your perfectly stable feed 'wrong,' and the only warning was a listserv email. These feeds also carry legal weight — immunization and reportable-condition submission is a compliance obligation, not a courtesy — so silent failure is a regulatory exposure, not just a data gap.</p><p>The engineering discipline this teaches generalizes: <strong>an interface isn't healthy because messages leave; it's healthy because acknowledgments come back clean.</strong> ACK monitoring — tracking accept/reject rates and alerting on threshold breaches — is the difference between finding this Tuesday afternoon and finding it three days later on a dashboard. And rejected messages always need explicit resubmission handling: like the deferred-queue principle elsewhere, every bounced VXU is a real child's real vaccination that the state registry doesn't know about until you send it again.</p>",
pit:["<strong>Fixing forward only:</strong> updating the mapping and letting three days of rejected immunizations evaporate — the backlog resubmission IS the compliance obligation.","<strong>The 'not patient-facing' shrug:</strong> deprioritizing because no clinician is complaining — registry gaps surface later as school-enrollment problems and public-health blind spots with your organization's name on them.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"An interface is healthy when the ACKs are clean, not when messages leave — I monitor reject rates on public-health feeds specifically because the other side changes on government timelines and tells you in a newsletter.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"MyChart",t:"Parent lost access to their 13-year-old's chart",d:"Furious parent called the clinic: their MyChart proxy access to their child's record 'suddenly broke' last week. Clinic staff don't know what to tell them and filed it as a portal defect.",
tri:["First question isn't 'what broke' — it's 'what birthday just happened?' Age-based proxy transitions fire automatically at policy-defined thresholds.","What does your organization's adolescent-proxy policy actually provide at this age — full removal, limited proxy, teen self-access?","Is the real deliverable a fix, or a script the clinic can use for this conversation — because this call recurs weekly forever."],
res:["Confirm the mechanism: the child crossed the age threshold (commonly 12–13) where automated proxy transition downgraded parental access — this is configuration executing policy, and the policy is executing law.","Verify the transition applied correctly per your org's model: typically limited parental proxy (scheduling, immunizations) plus optional teen self-access, with confidential categories shielded.","Equip the clinic with the explanation and the path: why the change happened (adolescent confidentiality law), what access remains, how the teen enrolls in self-access — delivered as a reusable script, not a one-off answer.","Do NOT restore full parental access as a courtesy — if the workflow misfired technically, fix the workflow; if it worked as designed, the design is the law's."],
kn:"<p><strong>Adolescent proxy configuration is state law rendered as build.</strong> Minor-consent statutes give adolescents confidentiality rights over specific care categories — reproductive health, mental health, substance use vary by state — and a portal that shows parents everything would expose legally protected information. So organizations configure automated proxy transitions at age thresholds: full parental access downgrades to a limited view, the teen gains optional self-access, and confidential categories are shielded. The parent experiences this as 'the portal broke'; the system experienced it as compliance.</p><p>The analyst skill this ticket exercises is <strong>distinguishing working-as-designed from working-as-desired, and equipping the front line for the gap between them.</strong> The clinic's helplessness is the actual defect here: they filed a legal design decision as a bug because nobody armed them with the explanation. The highest-leverage deliverable is the reusable script — what changed, why, what the parent still has, what the teen can do — because this exact call arrives every week as another cohort crosses the threshold. And the bright line stands: restoring full access as a favor isn't customer service, it's a confidentiality violation with your login on it.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The compassionate violation:</strong> restoring full parental proxy because the parent is upset and the request sounds reasonable — you've just overridden a legal confidentiality protection by ticket.","<strong>Answering once:</strong> resolving this call without leaving the clinic a script — the same conversation returns next week with a different family and the same helpless staff.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Proxy configuration is state law rendered as build — 'the portal broke' tickets at age 13 are usually policy working correctly, and my deliverable is the script that lets front-line staff explain it, not an exception.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Mobility",t:"Rover sessions dropping as nurses walk the hallway",d:"Med/surg nurses report Rover kicks them to the login screen mid–med pass when they walk from one end of the unit to the other. It's happened enough that some have gone back to the WOW.",
tri:["Map the drops physically: which hallways, which room ranges? Location-correlated drops point at wireless roaming, not the app or the device.","All devices in the zone or specific ones? All = infrastructure (AP handoff); some = device fleet (wifi drivers, battery/power states).","Any recent WLAN changes — controller updates, AP replacements, channel plan adjustments?"],
res:["Walk the route with a device and the network team — literally: reproduce the drop while watching AP association logs, correlating each session loss to a roaming handoff between access points.","The typical mechanism: a roaming handoff that takes longer than the app's session tolerance, or an AP with weak overlap coverage creating a dead zone the floor plan doesn't show — fixes range from roaming configuration to session-timeout tuning to an added AP.","Coordinate the fix with network/desktop teams, then re-walk the exact med-pass route to verify before telling nursing it's resolved.","Track the workflow regression meanwhile: nurses reverting to WOWs is a barcode-scanning and efficiency loss — leadership should know mobility adoption is bleeding until this is fixed."],
kn:"<p><strong>Clinical mobility is a three-layer system — app, device, wireless — that succeeds or fails as one.</strong> Rover assumes continuous connectivity while physically moving through a building full of concrete, metal, and interference; every hallway walk is a series of AP-to-AP handoffs that must complete faster than the app's session tolerance. When the layers' assumptions misalign — a slow handoff meets a strict timeout — the nurse sees a login screen mid–med pass, and no single team's dashboard shows a problem.</p><p>Two lessons ride on this. First, <strong>mobility tickets are tested by walking, not from a desk</strong> — the network looks perfect from the IDF closet, and the failure only exists in motion along the actual clinical route. This is where field-service experience becomes an analyst superpower: knowing the floor, the dead corners, the workflow rhythm. Second, watch the behavioral signal: clinicians abandoning a tool (back to the WOW) is the loudest metric there is — adoption regression means the workflow has already voted, and every un-scanned barcode during the workaround is a small withdrawal from the safety bank.</p>",
pit:["<strong>Desk-testing:</strong> confirming 'wifi is fine' from a stationary check when the failure only exists in motion during AP handoffs — walk the med-pass route or you'll never see it.","<strong>Blaming the user or the app first:</strong> 'hold it differently' / 'reinstall Rover' — location-correlated drops across users are infrastructure until proven otherwise.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"I test mobility tickets by walking the actual med-pass route with the device — the network always looks fine from a desk, and my field-service years taught me the failure lives in the hallways, not the dashboards.\"</span>"]},

{p:3,m:"Pop Health",t:"Diabetes care-gap list flagging patients who already had their A1c",d:"Population health MA spent the morning calling 'overdue' diabetes patients — several annoyed patients had the lab done weeks ago, and it's right there in their charts. The outreach list is wrong.",
tri:["Data lag or logic error? Check the registry's refresh timing first (Caboodle-based registries run on ETL schedules) — but weeks-old results ruled out means this isn't lag.","Which result satisfies the metric — and is <em>every</em> lab code that produces an A1c mapped to it? New or changed lab codes are the classic silent breaker.","How long has this been wrong, and how much outreach has already gone to false gaps?"],
res:["Trace one false-gap patient end-to-end: the A1c result exists in the chart — follow it into the registry logic and find where the metric fails to see it.","The frequent culprit: a lab code change upstream (a new analyzer, a Beaker build update, a reference-lab switch) created a result component the registry's metric mapping doesn't include — the data is present, the mapping is blind to it.","Update the metric mapping, re-run the registry, and regenerate the outreach list; recall or annotate the outstanding false-gap outreach so the MA stops calling patients who did everything right.","Institutionalize the fix: any new or changed lab code gets a registry-mapping review as part of the lab change process — connect this to the same change-notification discipline from the interface world."],
kn:"<p><strong>Population health registries are computed views: inclusion rules plus metric mappings running over the data warehouse.</strong> A 'care gap' isn't observed — it's calculated, and the calculation is only as good as its mappings. When an upstream lab code changes and the mapping doesn't follow, the registry goes quietly blind to real results: the patient did the lab, the chart shows it, and the metric still screams 'overdue.' False gaps waste outreach staff and burn patient goodwill ('I already did this — don't you people read my chart?'); the mirror failure — missed real gaps — silently harms care. Both come from the same root: mappings that drifted from the data.</p><p>Notice the recurring shape: this is <strong>the same disease as the ORU filing failure and the registry rejections</strong> — an upstream identifier or code changed, and a downstream consumer that keyed on the old form broke silently. Interfaces break loudly (error queues); registries break quietly (wrong lists). The systemic prescription is identical too: change-notification discipline connecting lab/code changes to every downstream consumer, plus a validation habit of tracing one patient end-to-end whenever a computed list smells wrong. Pattern recognition across modules is what makes year two of this job faster than year one.</p>",
pit:["<strong>The manual patch:</strong> telling the MA to chart-check every patient before calling — you've converted a mapping defect into a permanent staffing tax and left the registry wrong for every other consumer of it.","<strong>Stopping at the symptom:</strong> fixing the mapping without asking what upstream change created the unmapped code — that change process will mint the next blind spot on its own schedule.","<span class='iv'><strong>Interview angle:</strong> \"Registry accuracy dies upstream — a care-gap list is a computed claim, so I validate it by tracing one patient end-to-end, and my permanent fix is wiring lab-code changes into a registry-mapping review, because interfaces break loudly but registries break quietly.\"</span>"]}

];
